<section xml:id="notes-week-05" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 5</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in week 5, from September 28 to October 2.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 9/28</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Perform algebraic operations on functions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Relate the graph and domain of combined functions to the component functions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Compute and simplify the difference quotient of a function.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Algebra of Functions</title>

    <p>
        Suppose you had two functions:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>P(x)</m> - The price of the new base-level iPhone in year x, and 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>M(x)</m> - The amount of storage space in the new base-level iPhone in year x.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        If I want to make a new function, <m>C(x)</m>, which is the cost per gigabyte of storage in the new iPhone, how would I do that?
    </p> 

    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
        <nbsp />
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        Well, I need to divide <m>P(x)</m> by <m>M(x)</m>. I can do this at each point, or I can define a new function. It turns out these are the same thing. Algebra of functions means adding, subtracting, multiplying, dividing to get new functions.
    </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            So how do we do this? In the only way we really can; by operating on the outputs.
        </p>
        <example workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = 3x-2</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 + x + 1</m>. Compute each of the following:
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(2) + g(2)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(5) - g(5)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(1)g(1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\frac{f(-2)}{g(-2)}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
        
        </example>
        <p>
            In order for any of this to work, we need the value that we plug in to be in the domain of both component functions. 
        </p>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <definition xml:id="def-algebra-functions">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be two functions and <m>x</m> be any value in the domain of both <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>. Then,
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be two functions and <m>x</m> be any value in the domain of both <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>. Then, f+g, f-g, fg f/g.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <example workspace="0.1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = 2 - \sqrt{x+5}</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 + x - 2</m>. Compute and find expressions for
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(f+g)(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(fg)(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(f+g)(4)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(f+g)(-9)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(fg)(-1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(fg)(0)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Graphical Representation</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                What does the graph of <m>f+g</m> look like? How would you plot points on the graph?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                What does the graph of <m>f+g</m> look like? How would you plot points on the graph? Add the heights.
            </p>

