    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Domains of Combined Functions</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                As mentioned before, both functions individually need to be defined for the combined function to be defined. There's one additional constraint:
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                As mentioned before, both functions individually need to be defined for the combined function to be defined. There's one additional constraint: if we divide, the bottom can't be zero.
            </p>

            <example workspace="0.1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = 2 - \sqrt{x+5}</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 + x - 2</m>. Write an expression for each function below and find its domain.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>g(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(f+g)(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{f}{g}(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{g}{f}(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            </example>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x+3}</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 + 2x - 8</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the domain of <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, <m>f+g</m>, <m>\frac{f}{g}</m>, and <m>\frac{g}{f}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>(f+g)(1)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>(f-g)(3)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>(fg)(-4)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>\left(\frac{g}{f}\right)(2)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>\left(\frac{g}{f}\right)(6)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>\left(\frac{f}{g}\right)(2)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x+4}</m>, <m>g(x) = 1 + \frac{1}{x}</m>, and <m>h(x) = x^3 - x</m>. Find a formula for and state the domain of each of the following.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    <m>f+g</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f\cdot f</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>gh</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f-h</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x+3)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>g(x-2)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The Difference Quotient</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                When talking about linear functions, we discussed how the slope can be thought of as the rate of change of the function. What can we do if we want to talk about rates of change of non-linear functions?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                When talking about linear functions, we discussed how the slope can be thought of as the rate of change of the function. What can we do if we want to talk about rates of change of non-linear functions? Pick two points, and find the slope of the line between them. Consider two points that are distance <m>h</m> apart...
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Construct and simplify the difference quotient for the function
                    <md>
                        f(x) = 5x-2
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

